  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "## TEMPLATE 17: NM_DOPAMINERGIC_NOVELTY_REWARD_001\n**\"Architectural novelty \u2192 dopaminergic response \u2192 wanting/approach\"**\n\n**Structural pattern**: novelty_detection \u2192 reward_signal \u2192 motivated_approach\n**Transferable to**: Any novel stimulus domain\n\n**Causal chain**:\n1. Novel architectural features (unusual form, unexpected material, surprising spatial configuration) \u2192 hippocampal novelty signal (comparator function: mismatch between current input and stored representations)\n   - Level: ecological \u2192 circuit\n   - Bridging quality: HIGH\n   - Maturity: **how-actually** (Lisman & Grace, 2005)\n2. Hippocampal novelty signal \u2192 VTA dopamine release \u2192 mesolimbic activation (nucleus accumbens)\n   - Level: circuit\n   - Bridging quality: HIGH\n   - Maturity: **how-actually** (Bunzeck & D\u00fczel, 2006)\n3. Mesolimbic dopamine \u2192 \"wanting\" (incentive salience) \u2192 approach behavior and exploration\n   - Level: circuit \u2192 psychological \u2192 behavioral\n   - Bridging quality: MEDIUM\n   - Maturity: **how-actually** for wanting/approach (Berridge & Robinson, 2016); **how-plausibly** for the distinction between wanting (dopaminergic) and liking (opioidergic) in architectural contexts\n\n**NOTE**: Wanting \u2260 liking. Novel architecture may produce strong approach behavior (wanting to explore) without necessarily producing hedonic pleasure (liking the space). This dissociation is predicted by Berridge's incentive salience theory and is testable.\n\n**Scope conditions**: Novelty response habituates with repeated exposure (~3-5 exposures). The response is modulated by arousal state (low arousal \u2192 stronger novelty seeking; already-aroused \u2192 reduced novelty seeking). Connects to NM_NORADRENERGIC_EXPLORE_006 \u2014 LC-NE state modulates the threshold for novelty-driven exploration.\n**Overall maturity**: **how-actually** for the neural pathway; **how-plausibly** for specific architectural applications\n\n**Key references**: Lisman & Grace (2005, cited ~2,000); Bunzeck & D\u00fczel (2006, cited ~1,000); Berridge & Robinson (2016, cited ~1,500)\n\n---",
